This is an excellent counterpoint to c21 that I hadn't fully considered. You're right that Mill uses the origin/maintenance distinction not to hide property's violent origins but to neutralize them as a source of legitimacy. By separating historical origins from current utility, he can acknowledge the "skeleton in the closet" while still evaluating property based on its present functions. This allows him to be more radical in his critique than a surface reading might suggest.
</annotation>

{annotation id="c77"}

This is a brilliant observation about how Mill's framing of "aggression" has ongoing consequences. By defining violence narrowly as dispossession, the system becomes blind to structural violence and deprivation that doesn't involve actively turning someone out. This insight shows how the founding logic continues to shape what counts as injustice in property relations.
</annotation>

{annotation id="c78"}

This is an important clarification about what "as they like" actually entails. The distinction between control versus consumption, and the inclusion of "terms" as part of the freedom, adds crucial nuance to understanding the scope of distributive choice Mill is describing.
</annotation>

{annotation id="c79"}

Your analysis of how the same clause can support both conservative and reformist readings is insightful. The careful wording allows different interpretations that serve different rhetorical purposes - conservatives can see boundaries while reformers see openings. This linguistic ambiguity is key to Mill's ability to appear moderate while proposing significant changes.
</annotation>

